/T (Across the reporting period, 765 new peer connections were formed through the platform. A "connection" is defined as two students who have attended 2+ activities together. The average connected pair shares 3.4 activities, suggesting relationships that extend beyond single encounters.)
/E (Across the reporting period, 765 new peer connections were formed through the platform. A "connection" is defined as two students who have attended 2+ activities together. The average connected pair shares 3.4 activities, suggesting relationships that extend beyond single encounters.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (78% of connections are between students on different courses, indicating the platform facilitates cross-disciplinary social integration that would not typically occur through academic provision alone. This is particularly relevant for TEF evidence around co-curricular enrichment.)
/E (78% of connections are between students on different courses, indicating the platform facilitates cross-disciplinary social integration that would not typically occur through academic provision alone. This is particularly relevant for TEF evidence around co-curricular enrichment.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (1. Commuter student gap: Introduce lunchtime activity slots and promote Study category activities to commuter students through course-level communications. 2. Year 3 decline: Partner with the Careers Service and dissertation supervisors to promote final-year study groups and dissertation peer support sessions. 3. January re-engagement: Expand January Welcome activities for Semester 2 starters, using Reaction as the primary tool for social integration of late arrivals. 4. Cross-group deepening: Introduce themed cross-cultural activity events \(e.g. international cooking sessions, multilingual board game nights\) to further increase cross-group interaction rates. 5. Data integration: Cross-reference Reaction engagement data with VLE attendance and module outcomes to investigate whether platform engagement correlates with academic continuation and achievement.)
/E (1. Commuter student gap: Introduce lunchtime activity slots and promote Study category activities to commuter students through course-level communications. 2. Year 3 decline: Partner with the Careers Service and dissertation supervisors to promote final-year study groups and dissertation peer support sessions. 3. January re-engagement: Expand January Welcome activities for Semester 2 starters, using Reaction as the primary tool for social integration of late arrivals. 4. Cross-group deepening: Introduce themed cross-cultural activity events \(e.g. international cooking sessions, multilingual board game nights\) to further increase cross-group interaction rates. 5. Data integration: Cross-reference Reaction engagement data with VLE attendance and module outcomes to investigate whether platform engagement correlates with academic continuation and achievement.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (Students who begin with Sport activities subsequently engage with Study activities at a rate of 34%, compared to 12% for the general student population. This suggests that informal sporting activity serves as a gateway to academic peer support, with students building confidence and social networks through low-stakes physical activity before applying those connections to academic contexts.)
/E (Students who begin with Sport activities subsequently engage with Study activities at a rate of 34%, compared to 12% for the general student population. This suggests that informal sporting activity serves as a gateway to academic peer support, with students building confidence and social networks through low-stakes physical activity before applying those connections to academic contexts.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 48 0 R
/T (Students who engaged in 3+ categories \(sport AND study AND board games\) reported the highest belonging scores \(4.6/5\), compared to 3.9/5 for single-category users. This suggests that breadth of engagement, not just frequency, is associated with stronger belonging outcomes. The platform's multi-category design may therefore contribute to belonging in ways that single-activity provisions \(e.g. sport-only or study-only\) do not.)
/E (Students who engaged in 3+ categories \(sport AND study AND board games\) reported the highest belonging scores \(4.6/5\), compared to 3.9/5 for single-category users. This suggests that breadth of engagement, not just frequency, is associated with stronger belonging outcomes. The platform's multi-category design may therefore contribute to belonging in ways that single-activity provisions \(e.g. sport-only or study-only\) do not.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
